1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,178
Welcome to lesson twenty-three: extending Kubernetes.

2
00:00:04,278 --> 00:00:17,648
You'll add your own resource types, write a controller that makes them do something, add admission rules, and learn the other extension points. Many tools you've used, like cert-manager and Argo CD, are built exactly this way.

3
00:00:18,998 --> 00:00:21,600
Kubernetes has many extension points.

4
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:30,958
A CRD adds a new kind of object. A controller makes it do something. Admission policies and webhooks validate or change objects as they're created.

5
00:00:31,058 --> 00:00:38,108
Before building anything, check whether an existing operator already does it. Every custom controller is software you must maintain.

6
00:00:39,458 --> 00:00:50,348
A CRD teaches the API server a new resource type. Once created, you can get, apply and delete it, use RBAC on it, and watch it, just like built-in types.

7
00:00:50,448 --> 00:00:55,562
It defines a group, a kind, and versions. One version is the storage version.

8
00:00:55,662 --> 00:01:02,888
The status subresource separates what users write from what controllers write, and printer columns make kubectl get useful.

9
00:01:04,238 --> 00:01:11,432
The schema validates objects with types, required fields, patterns, minimums, maximums and defaults.

10
00:01:11,532 --> 00:01:18,166
CEL rules can check across fields, and transition rules using old self can make a field immutable after creation.

11
00:01:18,266 --> 00:01:25,796
Unknown fields are silently dropped. And be careful: deleting a CRD deletes every object of that type in the whole cluster.

12
00:01:27,146 --> 00:01:33,972
A CRD alone is just stored data. A controller watches those objects and makes reality match them.

13
00:01:34,072 --> 00:01:41,890
An operator is a controller that encodes an expert's knowledge for one application: how to install it, upgrade it, back it up and recover it.

14
00:01:41,990 --> 00:01:47,952
Most operators are written in Go with Kubebuilder, but there are frameworks for Python, Java and Rust too.

15
00:01:49,302 --> 00:01:51,344
Here's a reconciler in Go.

16
00:01:51,444 --> 00:02:00,078
It reads the Website, builds the desired Deployment, sets an owner reference, and applies it. Returning an error means the framework retries with backoff.

17
00:02:00,178 --> 00:02:04,364
Then it reports what it observed in the status, through the status subresource.

18
00:02:05,714 --> 00:02:08,108
Good controllers follow some rules.

19
00:02:08,208 --> 00:02:14,346
Reconcile the whole state every time, and make it safe to repeat. Keep no important state in memory.

20
00:02:14,446 --> 00:02:23,064
Set owner references, so children are cleaned up automatically. Use finalizers only for cleanup outside Kubernetes, and always remove them.

21
00:02:23,164 --> 00:02:30,662
Report what you observe in the status, return errors for retries, use least-privilege permissions, and run with leader election.

22
00:02:32,012 --> 00:02:35,878
For admission rules, prefer CEL policies when they're enough.

23
00:02:35,978 --> 00:02:44,452
A ValidatingAdmissionPolicy runs inside the API server. There's no extra service, and no risk of a crashed webhook blocking the cluster.

24
00:02:44,552 --> 00:02:47,202
Roll it out with warn first, then deny.

25
00:02:48,552 --> 00:02:57,570
When you need arbitrary code, use an admission webhook. The API server sends your service an admission review, and waits for allow, deny, or a patch.

26
00:02:57,670 --> 00:03:05,456
Webhooks are a very common cause of outages. If a webhook is down with failure policy Fail, every matching request is rejected.

27
00:03:05,556 --> 00:03:13,502
Never intercept your own namespace or kube-system, run at least two replicas, keep timeouts short, and automate certificates.

28
00:03:14,852 --> 00:03:20,654
In the lab, you'll create a Website CRD and see validation reject bad objects.

29
00:03:20,754 --> 00:03:28,924
Then you'll run a tiny controller that creates a ConfigMap, Deployment and Service for each Website, reconciles changes, and repairs drift.

30
00:03:29,024 --> 00:03:36,042
You'll watch garbage collection clean up, write a kubectl plugin, and see how a broken webhook blocks Pod creation.

31
00:03:37,392 --> 00:03:38,538
Let's recap.

32
00:03:38,638 --> 00:03:45,144
A CRD adds a new type, a controller gives it behavior, and an operator automates expert knowledge.

33
00:03:45,244 --> 00:03:53,318
Validate with schemas and CEL, write level-triggered controllers with owner references, and prefer CEL admission policies over webhooks.

34
00:03:53,418 --> 00:03:59,540
Next, the final lesson: multi-cluster, multi-tenancy and platform engineering.
